WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.647
Agent security needs ongoing testing because prompts, models, tools, retrieval sources, and workflows change.

2
00:00:07.647 --> 00:00:20.313
Threat modeling and abuse-case review help identify which actions are sensitive, which data sources are untrusted, where authorization is enforced, and which failure modes matter most.

3
00:00:20.313 --> 00:00:30.748
Tool permission review should be part of any change that adds a capability, expands credentials, changes retrieval scope, or modifies approval behavior.

4
00:00:30.748 --> 00:00:36.165
Regression tests should cover prompts, retrieval, memory, and tool workflows.

5
00:00:36.165 --> 00:00:50.105
Useful tests can check authorization before retrieval, argument validation before tool execution, denied actions, approval gates, memory isolation, stale content handling, and safe output formatting.

6
00:00:50.105 --> 00:01:02.770
Evaluation datasets can be used defensively to track whether the agent remains aligned with expected behavior across model or prompt changes, without relying on manual spot checks alone.

7
00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:09.462
Logging should make agent behavior understandable without exposing unnecessary sensitive data.

8
00:01:09.462 --> 00:01:17.029
Logs may include prompts, retrieval events, tool calls, approvals, denials, outputs, and validation results.

9
00:01:17.029 --> 00:01:31.368
Redaction, data minimization, retention limits, and access controls are essential because these records can contain personal data, secrets, source code, customer information, or sensitive business context.

10
00:01:31.368 --> 00:01:41.006
Audit trails should clearly show who requested an action, what the model proposed, what policy allowed or denied, and what the system executed.

11
00:01:41.006 --> 00:01:45.467
Monitoring and incident response turn controls into operations.

12
00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:59.009
Watch for unusual tool use, repeated denials, sensitive-data access, retrieval anomalies, cost spikes, unexpected destinations, memory changes, provider failures, and repeated unsafe requests.

13
00:01:59.009 --> 00:02:08.488
Incident response should cover prompt injection, data leakage, unsafe actions, memory contamination, tool misuse, and provider outages.

14
00:02:08.488 --> 00:02:16.454
After an incident, update prompts, policies, tests, logs, tool scopes, and runbooks so the agent gets safer over time.
